\label{ch:subtyping}

Subtyping is the principle that a value of one type can be used wherever a value of another type is expected. It is the theoretical foundation of object-oriented programming, where a \texttt{Dog} can be used wherever an \texttt{Animal} is expected, and it pervades modern type systems in the form of structural subtyping, row polymorphism, and coercion. This chapter develops the theory of subtyping, the concept of variance, and the implementation techniques that make subtyping practical in compilers.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 46.1
%% ============================================================
\section{Subtyping Rules}\label{sec:subtyping-rules}

A subtyping relation $S <: T$ (read ``$S$ is a subtype of $T$'') asserts that every value of type $S$ can be used as a value of type $T$. The relation is a preorder: it is reflexive and transitive.

\subsection{Basic Rules}\label{sec:basic-subtyping}

\begin{typerules}{Basic Subtyping}
  \typeaxiom{Refl}{\Gamma \vdash \tau <: \tau} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Trans}{\Gamma \vdash \tau_1 <: \tau_2 \quad \Gamma \vdash \tau_2 <: \tau_3}
            {\Gamma \vdash \tau_1 <: \tau_3} \\[6pt]
  \typeaxiom{Top}{\Gamma \vdash \tau <: \top} \\[6pt]
  \typeaxiom{Bot}{\Gamma \vdash \bot <: \tau}
\end{typerules}

The type $\top$ (top) is the supertype of all types; the type $\bot$ (bottom) is the subtype of all types. In most languages, $\top$ corresponds to \texttt{Object} or \texttt{Any}, and $\bot$ corresponds to the type of non-termination or exceptions.

\subsection{Subsumption}\label{sec:subsumption}

The connection between subtyping and typing is the \emph{subsumption rule}:

\[
  \typerule{Subsumption}{\Gamma \vdash e : \tau_1 \quad \Gamma \vdash \tau_1 <: \tau_2}
            {\Gamma \vdash e : \tau_2}
\]

This rule allows a value of type $\tau_1$ to be used wherever type $\tau_2$ is expected, provided $\tau_1$ is a subtype of $\tau_2$.

\subsection{Function Subtyping}\label{sec:function-subtyping}

Function types are subtyped \emph{contravariantly} in their argument and \emph{covariantly} in their result:

\[
  \typerule{Arrow}{\Gamma \vdash \tau_1' <: \tau_1 \quad \Gamma \vdash \tau_2 <: \tau_2'}
            {\Gamma \vdash \tau_1 \to \tau_2 <: \tau_1' \to \tau_2'}
\]

This means that a function accepting a more general argument and returning a more specific result can be used where a function accepting a more specific argument and returning a more general result is expected.

\begin{proof}[Justification]
If $f : \tau_1 \to \tau_2$ and we need a function of type $\tau_1' \to \tau_2'$, we must show that $f$ can accept any argument of type $\tau_1'$ and produce a result of type $\tau_2'$. Since $\tau_1' <: \tau_1$, any value of type $\tau_1'$ can be passed to $f$. Since $\tau_2 <: \tau_2'$, the result of $f$ can be used as a value of type $\tau_2'$. \qed
\end{proof}

