\section{Variance: Covariance, Contravariance, and Invariance}\label{sec:variance}

Variance describes how subtyping between component types relates to subtyping between compound types.

\subsection{Definitions}\label{sec:variance-defs}

\begin{definition}[Variance]\label{def:variance}
Let $F$ be a type constructor (a function from types to types). The \emph{variance} of $F$ in its parameter is:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Covariant} if $S <: T \implies F(S) <: F(T)$.
  \item \textbf{Contravariant} if $S <: T \implies F(T) <: F(S)$.
  \item \textbf{Invariant} if neither covariance nor contravariance holds (in general).
  \item \textbf{Bivariant} if both covariance and contravariance hold (only in degenerate cases).
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Variance of common type constructors}\label{tab:variance-examples}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
\textbf{Type Constructor} & \textbf{Variance} & \textbf{Justification} \\
\hline
$\tau \to -$ (return position) & Covariant & Results flow outward \\
$- \to \tau$ (argument position) & Contravariant & Arguments flow inward \\
$\mathrm{List}<$-$>$ & Covariant & Elements are produced, not consumed \\
$\mathrm{Consumer}<$-$>$ & Contravariant & Elements are consumed, not produced \\
$\mathrm{Ref}<$-$>$ (mutable) & Invariant & Both read and write \\
$\mathrm{Array}<$-$>$ (mutable) & Invariant & Both read and write \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Variance in Java and C\#}\label{sec:variance-java}

Java's generics use \emph{use-site variance} (wildcards):
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{List<? extends Animal>} is covariant: you can read \texttt{Animal}s but not write.
  \item \texttt{List<? super Dog>} is contravariant: you can write \texttt{Dog}s but not read (except as \texttt{Object}).
  \item \texttt{List<Dog>} is invariant: you can both read and write \texttt{Dog}s.
\end{itemize}

C\# uses \emph{declaration-site variance}:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{IEnumerable<out T>} is covariant in \texttt{T}.
  \item \texttt{IComparer<in T>} is contravariant in \texttt{T}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Variance Checking}\label{sec:variance-checking}

For declaration-site variance, the compiler must verify that the declared variance is sound. The rule is:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A type parameter declared \textbf{covariant} (\texttt{out}) may appear only in \emph{output} positions (return types of methods).
  \item A type parameter declared \textbf{contravariant} (\texttt{in}) may appear only in \emph{input} positions (parameter types of methods).
  \item An \textbf{invariant} type parameter may appear in both positions.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Variance checking in Scala]\label{ex:scala-variance}
\begin{verbatim}
  trait Producer[+A] {          // covariant: A in output position only
    def get(): A
  }

  trait Consumer[-A] {          // contravariant: A in input position only
    def consume(a: A): Unit
  }

  trait Ref[A] {                // invariant: A in both positions
    def get(): A
    def set(a: A): Unit
  }
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

