\section{Record and Object Subtyping}\label{sec:record-subtyping}

Record and object types introduce structural subtyping: a record with more fields is a subtype of a record with fewer fields (provided the shared fields have compatible types).

\subsection{Width Subtyping}\label{sec:width-subtyping}

\begin{definition}[Width Subtyping]\label{def:width-subtyping}
A record type $\{l_1 : \tau_1, \ldots, l_n : \tau_n, \ldots, l_m : \tau_m\}$ is a subtype of $\{l_1 : \tau_1, \ldots, l_n : \tau_n\}$ (the same fields, possibly in a different order). That is, adding fields preserves subtyping.
\end{definition}

\[
  \typeaxiom{Width}{\{l_1 : \tau_1, \ldots, l_n : \tau_n, l_{n+1} : \tau_{n+1}, \ldots, l_m : \tau_m\} <: \{l_1 : \tau_1, \ldots, l_n : \tau_n\}}
\]

\subsection{Depth Subtyping}\label{sec:depth-subtyping}

\begin{definition}[Depth Subtyping]\label{def:depth-subtyping}
Record types are subtyped component-wise: if each field type of the first record is a subtype of the corresponding field type of the second, then the first record is a subtype of the second.
\end{definition}

\[
  \typerule{Depth}{\forall i.\, \tau_i <: \tau_i'}
            {\{l_1 : \tau_1, \ldots, l_n : \tau_n\} <: \{l_1 : \tau_1', \ldots, l_n : \tau_n'\}}
\]

\subsection{Permutation Subtyping}\label{sec:permutation-subtyping}

Record types are also subtyped up to field reordering:
\[
  \typeaxiom{Permute}{\{l_1 : \tau_1, \ldots, l_n : \tau_n\} = \{l_{\pi(1)} : \tau_{\pi(1)}, \ldots, l_{\pi(n)} : \tau_{\pi(n)}\} \text{ for permutation } \pi}
\]

\subsection{Object Subtyping}\label{sec:object-subtyping}

In object-oriented languages, subtyping combines width, depth, and permutation subtyping with \emph{method subtyping}: an object with more methods is a subtype of an object with fewer methods, provided the shared methods have compatible types (contravariant in arguments, covariant in return types).

\begin{example}[Object subtyping]\label{ex:object-subtyping}
Consider:
\begin{verbatim}
  class Animal { def speak(): String }
  class Dog extends Animal { def speak(): String = "Woof"; def fetch(): Unit }
\end{verbatim}
\texttt{Dog} is a subtype of \texttt{Animal} because it has all of \texttt{Animal}'s methods (with compatible types) plus additional ones.
\end{example}

