\section{Intersection and Union Types}\label{sec:intersection-union}

Intersection and union types extend the subtyping lattice with greatest lower bounds and least upper bounds.

\subsection{Intersection Types}\label{sec:intersection-types}

\begin{definition}[Intersection Type]\label{def:intersection-type}
The intersection type $\tau_1 \wedge \tau_2$ (also written $\tau_1 \mathbin{\&} \tau_2$) is the type of values that have \emph{both} type $\tau_1$ and type $\tau_2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{typerules}{Intersection Types}
  \typerule{Intro}{\Gamma \vdash e : \tau_1 \quad \Gamma \vdash e : \tau_2}
            {\Gamma \vdash e : \tau_1 \wedge \tau_2} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Elim-L}{\Gamma \vdash e : \tau_1 \wedge \tau_2}
            {\Gamma \vdash e : \tau_1} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Elim-R}{\Gamma \vdash e : \tau_1 \wedge \tau_2}
            {\Gamma \vdash e : \tau_2}
\end{typerules}

Intersection types arise naturally in type inference for overloaded functions and in the analysis of object-oriented programs.

\subsection{Union Types}\label{sec:union-types}

\begin{definition}[Union Type]\label{def:union-type}
The union type $\tau_1 \vee \tau_2$ (also written $\tau_1 \mid \tau_2$) is the type of values that have \emph{either} type $\tau_1$ or type $\tau_2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{typerules}{Union Types}
  \typerule{Intro-L}{\Gamma \vdash e : \tau_1}
            {\Gamma \vdash e : \tau_1 \vee \tau_2} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Intro-R}{\Gamma \vdash e : \tau_2}
            {\Gamma \vdash e : \tau_1 \vee \tau_2} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Elim}{\Gamma \vdash e : \tau_1 \vee \tau_2 \quad
                   \Gamma, x : \tau_1 \vdash e_1 : \tau \quad
                   \Gamma, x : \tau_2 \vdash e_2 : \tau}
            {\Gamma \vdash \mathtt{match}\; e\; \mathtt{with}\; x \to e_1 \mid x \to e_2 : \tau}
\end{typerules}

Union types appear in TypeScript, Ceylon, and increasingly in modern language designs. They provide a principled alternative to tagged unions for handling heterogeneous data.

\subsection{Distributivity}\label{sec:distributivity}

Intersection and union types distribute over function types:
\begin{align*}
  (\tau_1 \wedge \tau_2) \to \tau &\equiv (\tau_1 \to \tau) \vee (\tau_2 \to \tau) \\
  \tau \to (\tau_1 \vee \tau_2) &\equiv (\tau \to \tau_1) \vee (\tau \to \tau_2) \\
  (\tau_1 \vee \tau_2) \to \tau &\equiv (\tau_1 \to \tau) \wedge (\tau_2 \to \tau)
\end{align*}

These equivalences are the type-theoretic counterpart of the logical relationships between conjunction and disjunction.

