\section{Decidability of Subtyping}\label{sec:decidability}

The decidability of the subtyping relation depends on the type system features.

\subsection{Decidable Cases}\label{sec:decidable-cases}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Structural subtyping with records and variants.} Decidable by a simple recursive algorithm.
  \item \textbf{Nominal subtyping with single inheritance.} Decidable by following the inheritance chain.
  \item \textbf{Nominal subtyping with multiple inheritance.} Decidable if the inheritance graph is a DAG (no cycles); undecidable in the presence of certain forms of mixin composition.
  \item \textbf{Subtyping with bounded quantification (F$_{<:}$).} Decidable for the kernel fragment; the full system's decidability was a long-standing open problem, resolved by Pierce~\cite{pierce1992}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Undecidable Cases}\label{sec:undecidable-cases}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Subtyping with recursive types and contravariance.} The combination of equi-recursive types and contravariant function types can lead to undecidability~\cite{pierce1992}.
  \item \textbf{Subtyping with intersection types and unrestricted use.} Full intersection type systems can encode arbitrary computations, making subtyping undecidable.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Algorithmic Subtyping}\label{sec:algorithmic-subtyping}

For decidable subtyping relations, an \emph{algorithmic} presentation eliminates the transitivity rule (which is non-syntax-directed) and replaces it with syntax-directed rules:

\begin{typerules}{Algorithmic Subtyping for Records}
  \typeaxiom{Top}{\Gamma \vdash \tau <: \top} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Refl}{\overline{\Gamma \vdash \alpha <: \alpha}}{for type variables} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Record}{\forall l_i \in \{l_1', \ldots, l_m'\}.\, \exists l_j \in \{l_1, \ldots, l_n\}.\, l_i = l_j \wedge \Gamma \vdash \tau_j <: \tau_i'}
            {\Gamma \vdash \{l_1 : \tau_1, \ldots, l_n : \tau_n\} <: \{l_1' : \tau_1', \ldots, l_m' : \tau_m'\}} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Arrow}{\Gamma \vdash \tau_1' <: \tau_1 \quad \Gamma \vdash \tau_2 <: \tau_2'}
            {\Gamma \vdash \tau_1 \to \tau_2 <: \tau_1' \to \tau_2'}
\end{typerules}

\begin{theorem}[Equivalence of Declarative and Algorithmic Subtyping]\label{thm:algo-subtyping}
The algorithmic subtyping relation coincides with the declarative relation (with reflexivity and transitivity) for all standard type systems.
\end{theorem}

