\section{Implementation: Coercion vs. Subsumption}\label{sec:coercion-vs-subsumption}

There are two fundamentally different ways to implement subtyping in a compiler.

\subsection{Coercion-Based Implementation}\label{sec:coercion-impl}

In the coercion-based approach, subtyping is implemented by inserting explicit \emph{coercion} operations that convert a value from the subtype to the supertype.

\begin{example}[Coercion for subtyping]\label{ex:coercion}
If $\mathrm{int} <: \mathrm{float}$, then the expression $3.14 + 42$ is compiled as $3.14 + \mathrm{int\_to\_float}(42)$. The coercion $\mathrm{int\_to\_float}$ is an explicit conversion function.
\end{example}

Coercions can change the runtime representation: an \texttt{int} (32 bits) is converted to a \texttt{float} (64 bits). This is sometimes called \emph{coercive subtyping} or \emph{conversion-based subtyping}.

\subsection{Subsumption-Based Implementation}\label{sec:subsumption-impl}

In the subsumption-based approach, subtyping is a purely static relation: a value of the subtype \emph{is} a value of the supertype, with no runtime conversion. This requires that the runtime representation of the subtype be compatible with the supertype.

\begin{example}[Subsumption for objects]\label{ex:subsumption}
If \texttt{Dog} $<:$ \texttt{Animal}, then a \texttt{Dog} object can be used wherever an \texttt{Animal} is expected, because the \texttt{Dog} object's memory layout includes all the fields and methods of \texttt{Animal}. No conversion is needed; only the static type changes.
\end{example}

\subsection{Comparison}\label{sec:coercion-vs-subsumption-comparison}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Coercion vs. subsumption}\label{tab:coercion-subsumption}
\begin{tabular}{p{3cm}p{5cm}p{5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Coercion} & \textbf{Subsumption} \\
\hline
Runtime cost & Conversion function call & None (static only) \\
Representation change & Yes & No \\
Example & $\mathrm{int} <: \mathrm{float}$ & $\mathrm{Dog} <: \mathrm{Animal}$ \\
Transitivity & Compose coercions & Transitive relation \\
Type safety & Must verify round-trip & Guaranteed by layout \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Hybrid Approaches}\label{sec:hybrid}

Many languages use a hybrid approach:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Java.} Uses subsumption for reference types (no conversion needed) but coercion for primitive types (\texttt{int} to \texttt{long}).
  \item \textbf{TypeScript.} Uses subsumption for structural types (no runtime representation) since TypeScript erases all types.
  \item \textbf{Scala.} Uses subsumption for class hierarchies but implicit conversions (a form of coercion) for ad hoc subtyping relationships.
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
The theory of subtyping was developed in the context of the FORTE project at Carnegie Mellon in the late 1980s and early 1990s. Cardelli's work on Amber~\cite{cardelli1988} introduced the first comprehensive subtyping theory for object-oriented languages. Pierce's study of F$_{<:}$~\cite{pierce1992} revealed the subtle interactions between bounded quantification and subtyping, leading to the distinction between kernel and full F$_{<:}$. The modern understanding of variance, due to Igarashi and Pierce~\cite{igarashi2001}, provides a clean framework for reasoning about generic types in the presence of subtyping.
\end{historical}

