\section{Advanced Topics in Subtyping}\label{sec:subtyping-advanced}

\subsection{Decidability of Algorithmic Subtyping}\label{sec:algo-subtyping-decidable}

The algorithmic subtyping relation must be decidable for the type checker to terminate. The key results are:

\begin{theorem}[Decidability for Structural Subtyping]\label{thm:dec-structural}
Algorithmic subtyping for structural types (records, variants, functions) is decidable in polynomial time. The algorithm proceeds by structural recursion on the types.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Decidability for F$_{<:}$]\label{thm:dec-fsub}
The kernel fragment of F$_{<:}$ (bounded quantification with subtyping) has a decidable subtyping relation. The full system's decidability was established by Pierce~\cite{pierce1992} through a careful analysis of the interaction between bounded quantification and contravariance.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch for kernel F$_{<:}$]
The kernel fragment restricts the subtyping rule for universal types to be syntax-directed:
\[
  \typerule{Forall-Kernel}{\Gamma \vdash \tau_1 <: \sigma_1 \quad \Gamma, \alpha <: \sigma_1 \vdash \tau_2 <: \tau_1[\alpha/\beta]}
            {\Gamma \vdash \forall \alpha <: \tau_1.\, \tau_2 <: \forall \beta <: \sigma_1.\, \tau_1}
\]
This restriction eliminates the need for the non-syntax-directed ``all'' rule, making the algorithm terminating. \qed
\end{proof}

\subsection{Recursive Types and Subtyping}\label{sec:recursive-subtyping}

Recursive types (equi-recursive or iso-recursive) interact subtly with subtyping.

\begin{definition}[Equi-Recursive Subtyping]\label{def:equi-recursive-sub}
For equi-recursive types, $\mu \alpha.\, \tau$ is identified with $\tau[\mu \alpha.\, \tau / \alpha]$. Subtyping is defined on the infinite unfoldings:
\[
  \mu \alpha.\, \tau <: \mu \beta.\, \sigma \iff \mathrm{unfold}(\mu \alpha.\, \tau) <: \mathrm{unfold}(\mu \beta.\, \sigma)
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Recursive record types]\label{ex:recursive-record}
A linked list node can be typed as a recursive record:
\[
  \mathrm{Node} = \mu \alpha.\, \{\mathrm{val} : \mathrm{int},\, \mathrm{next} : \alpha\}
\]
The subtyping $\mathrm{Node} <: \{\mathrm{val} : \mathrm{int}\}$ holds by width subtyping on the unfolded type.
\end{example}

\begin{caution}
The combination of equi-recursive types and contravariant function types can make subtyping undecidable. The issue is that the subtyping check for $\mu \alpha.\, \tau_1 <: \mu \beta.\, \tau_2$ may generate an infinite sequence of unfolding steps that never terminates. Pierce's analysis identifies the precise conditions under which this occurs.
\end{caution}

\subsection{Intersection and Union Types in Practice}\label{sec:intersection-union-practice}

Intersection and union types have found increasing use in modern programming languages.

\begin{example}[TypeScript intersection types]\label{ex:ts-intersection}
\begin{verbatim}
  type Named = { name: string };
  type Aged = { age: number };
  type Person = Named & Aged;  // intersection: has both name and age

  function greet(p: Person) {
    console.log(`Hello, ${p.name}, age ${p.age}`);
  }
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\begin{example}[TypeScript union types with narrowing]\label{ex:ts-union}
\begin{verbatim}
  type Result = Success | Failure;
  type Success = { ok: true; value: string };
  type Failure = { ok: false; error: string };

  function handle(r: Result) {
    if (r.ok) {
      // TypeScript narrows r to Success
      console.log(r.value);
    } else {
      // TypeScript narrows r to Failure
      console.log(r.error);
    }
  }
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Flow Typing and Subtyping}\label{sec:flow-typing}

\emph{Flow typing} (as in TypeScript and Flow) combines subtyping with \emph{type narrowing}: the type of a variable can be refined at specific program points based on control-flow analysis.

\begin{verbatim}
  let x: string | number = getValue();
  if (typeof x === "string") {
    // x : string (narrowed from string | number)
    x.toUpperCase();
  } else {
    // x : number (narrowed from string | number)
    x.toFixed(2);
  }
\end{verbatim}

\begin{definition}[Type Narrowing]\label{def:type-narrowing}
\emph{Type narrowing} is the process of refining a variable's type at a program point based on a condition. If $x : \tau_1 \vee \tau_2$ and a test confirms $x : \tau_1$, then $x$ is narrowed to $\tau_1$ in the then-branch and to $\tau_2$ in the else-branch.
\end{definition}

