\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:summary-46}

Subtyping and variance are fundamental to the design of modern type systems. The subtyping relation determines which types are interchangeable, while variance determines how subtyping propagates through type constructors. The implementation of subtyping---whether by coercion or by subsumption---has profound implications for runtime performance and type safety.

For further reading, we recommend Cardelli's type systems tutorial~\cite{cardelli1997}, Pierce's comprehensive treatment~\cite{pierce2002}, and the collected papers on subtyping edited by Pierce~\cite{pierce2000}.
